 ],
 "weightings": {
  "hit": "Each hit is one pair.",
  "hit_shower": "Each hit is one pair; the shower's value repeats on every one of its hits, so a shower counts in proportion to its hits, which grow with its momentum.",
  "shower_by_hits": "Both are per-shower values, aggregated from the event table with each shower weighted by its hit count, which reproduces the hit-level value; the per-shower value, each shower counted once, is given beside it."
 },
 "notes": [
  "Hits are not independent pairs: p, θ and φ take one value per shower, repeated on every one of its hits, and the hits of one shower share its development. The sampling unit is the event. Intervals are a grouped jackknife on Fisher's z = atanh r over 64 event groups (Student-t on G − 1 degrees of freedom), quoted from 4 groups and |r| < 1. n_eff = 3 + 1/SE² is the number of independent pairs the interval is worth; se_iid = 1/√(N − 3) with N = 8,573 hits is the naive standard error a hit count would claim.",
  "φ is circular, so it has no linear correlation: no Σφ or Σφ² is accumulated, and its cells are hatched with r empty. They carry Mardia's circular–linear R of the other variable with (cos φ, sin φ), from 0 (no association) to 1, with no sign because a circle has no order, quoted from 8 distinct azimuths, and its jackknife standard error from 5 event groups. R is never negative: with no association R² still averages 2/(N − 1) over N showers each counted once (null_R2, N = 287), the reference for per_shower_R; a hit-weighted R counts a shower by its hits and has no such simple reference.",
  "Mardia's R needs 8 distinct azimuths; this selection has 4. Three directions fit any variable exactly (R = 1), and a few cannot constrain a dependence on the direction. The φ cells carry no R.",
  "p, θ and φ are per-shower values: a cell pairing one with a hit variable counts each shower in proportion to its hits, as the notebook's hit-level matrix does. Pairs of two per-shower values come from the event table, weighted by hits, which equals the hit-level value, with the per-shower value (each shower counted once) beside it.",
  "Hit energy is linear, as in the notebook: it spans about fifteen decades, so its r is set by the most energetic hits. energy_scale=log10 shows the bulk."
 ],
